First of all, I bloody love the first game. I love the lore, characters, atmosphere, and design. That's why I was so excited about Dishonored 2 that I got an art book for the first game, went to the Dishonored 2 Showcase in BAFTA and got myself Dishonored 2 Collector's edition. And still, this game manages to frustrate me. Why? Because of a wild mix of
- perfect level design and beautiful surroundings. Just to give you the right impression: I spent ten hours on the second level because I felt I needed to take a good look at everything that was in there, and I wasn't disappointed. Every room is interesting to explore, every street is picturesque in its own way. I tried all possible ways to go through the level and liked them all;
- wonderful music. I often listen to the Dishonored soundtrack, and the music in the second game is just as fitting;
- strong and multilayered connection to the first game. Many characters, details of environment and lore from Dishonored were used in the sequel. As a result, the transition between Dunwall and Karnaca, the first and the second game feels absolutely smooth;
- lore, lore everywhere! Books, songs, posters, people talking in the street — everything makes Karnaca feel alive;
And
- lazily written quests you can break easily if you decide not to follow the quest marker. I did it on the third level where I guessed the safe combination instead of getting it from the character, and things went weird after that: Emily kept doing what she should do according to the information she had but clearly had no idea what was going on. There are simply no monologue options for this scenario, which is disappointing. The game is made in a way that allows the player to choose from many paths that lead to the goal but at least one of them doesn't work as it should;
- fan fiction style dialogues. Cringy monologues some characters (including Emily) give all of a sudden that totally break the mood. A good example — a dialogue between Emily and Dalila's statue (bits I remembered):
Dalila: haha you can't hide
Emily: I did a thingy you won't like
Dalila: I hate you for that
Emily: I will take back what's mine and even make things better!
Seriously?..
If a character has a trait, he or she won't wait to show it to you in a simplest way possible. Meagan likes Sokolov? She will tell you this many times despite being described as an exceptionally reserved person. You found an alternative way to deal with a target? Don't worry, the protagonist will mention it at least twice. I also liked the fact that Emily, a witch and a bone charm maker in my playthrough, is more than eager to help the Abbey of the Everyman. As if they wouldn't burn her if they learn she has a connection to the Outsider.
- horrible, horrible optimisation, even after the latest patch;
- bugs: floating npcs, npcs that see you through walls, npcs that don't see you when you are standing in front of them or lose sight of you in the same situation.
The result? I bloody love the second game, hate it at the same time and will definitely have multiple satisfying and frustrating playthroughs.

